steps.body:
Before reading anything, predict what this prints. Write your answer down.

```cpp-snippet
int a = 5;
int* p = &a;
*p = *p + 10;
int b = *p;
b = 0;
std::cout << a << ' ' << b << ' ' << (p == &a) << '\n';
```

Then a second question: `int& r = a;` and `int* p = &a;` both let you change `a`. Guess one thing a pointer can do that a reference can't.

### What a pointer is

Every object your program creates lives at some address in memory. A **pointer** is a variable whose value is one of those addresses. `int* p` reads "p is a pointer to int": it holds the address of an `int`.

Two operators do all the work:

- `&x` (address-of) gives the address of `x`.
- `*p` (dereference) means "the object at the address in `p`". You can read it and assign to it.

So in the warm-up snippet, `p` holds the address of `a`, `*p = *p + 10` changes `a` to 15, `b` gets a **copy** of 15, and setting `b = 0` doesn't touch `a`. Output: `15 0 1`.

A pointer differs from a reference in three ways that matter: it can be **null** (`nullptr`, points at nothing), it can be **reseated** (point at something else later), and it supports **arithmetic** when it points into an array. A reference is fixed to one object for its whole life and is never null. The guideline you'll see in modern code: use a reference when "there is always exactly one object"; use a pointer when "maybe no object" or "walk across many objects".

### Pointers into arrays

An array's elements sit next to each other. If `p` points at element `i`, then `p + 1` points at element `i + 1` (the compiler multiplies by `sizeof(int)` for you). `*(p + k)` and `p[k]` are the same thing. The STL idea of a half-open range `[first, last)` comes straight from this: `first` points at the first element, `last` points **one past** the final element, and a loop runs `while (first != last)`. `std::vector`'s iterators are exactly this idea wrapped in a type.

### Where objects live: stack and heap

- **Stack (automatic storage).** Local variables and function parameters. Each function call pushes a *frame* holding its locals; when the function returns, the frame is gone and so are the objects. Allocation is just moving the stack pointer, so it is very fast. Size is limited (often 1–8 MB), so a local `int big[10'000'000]` can crash.
- **Heap (free store, dynamic storage).** Memory you ask for at run time with `new` (Wednesday's lesson). It lives until someone frees it, regardless of which function created it. `std::vector` keeps its elements on the heap and its small bookkeeping (pointer, size, capacity) wherever the vector object itself lives.
- **Static storage.** Globals and `static` locals: alive for the whole program.

The single most common pointer bug follows from this: a pointer that outlives the object it points to (a **dangling pointer**).

```cpp-snippet
int* make_number() {
    int n = 42;      // n lives in make_number's stack frame
    return &n;       // BUG: the frame is destroyed on return
}
// int* p = make_number(); *p is undefined behaviour: it may print 42, garbage, or crash.
```

Undefined behaviour is the C++ term for "the standard promises nothing". It often seems to work in a quick test, which is why it is dangerous. Compile with `g++ -std=c++20 -g -fsanitize=address file.cpp` while learning (on macOS `g++` is Apple clang, and the sanitizer works there): AddressSanitizer turns many of these into a clear error report, with the line that touched dead memory.

### `const` and pointers

Read declarations right to left:

- `const int* p`: pointer to a const int. You may move `p`, you may not write `*p`.
- `int* const p`: const pointer to int. You may write `*p`, you may not move `p`.

A function that only reads an array takes `const int*`, the same way it would take `const std::vector<int>&`.

### Worked example: walk a range with two pointers

```cpp
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

// Count values greater than t in [first, last).
std::size_t count_greater(const int* first, const int* last, int t) {
    std::size_t n = 0;
    for (; first != last; ++first)
        if (*first > t) ++n;
    return n;
}

void double_all(int* first, int* last) {
    while (first != last) {
        *first *= 2;   // write through the pointer
        ++first;       // move to the next element
    }
}

int main() {
    int data[] = {3, 9, 1, 12, 7};
    int* first = data;        // an array name decays to a pointer to its first element
    int* last = data + 5;     // one past the end
    std::cout << count_greater(first, last, 5) << '\n';   // 3 (9, 12, 7)
    double_all(first, last);
    for (int* p = first; p != last; ++p) std::cout << *p << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';                                     // 6 18 2 24 14
    int x = 10;
    int* px = &x;
    int* nothing = nullptr;
    std::cout << (nothing == nullptr) << ' ' << *px << '\n'; // 1 10
}
```

Two things to notice. `count_greater` takes `const int*` and the compiler would reject `*first = 0` inside it. And `last` is never dereferenced: it is only compared against. That is what makes an empty range (`first == last`) work with no special case, a habit you'll use on Monday's linked lists, where `nullptr` plays the role of `last`.

