assert(max_element_ptr(a, a) == nullptr);
*max_element_ptr(a, a + 4) = 0;
assert(a[1] == 0 && a[3] == 9);

steps.body:
In week 2 you wrote a linked list in Python, where the garbage collector cleaned up after you. In C++ every node is a heap object you `new` and must `delete`, and every arrow is a `Node*` you move by hand. The problems are the same; the bookkeeping is now visible.

The helpers every task below uses (also in the starter file):

```cpp-snippet
struct Node { int value; Node* next; };
Node* build(const std::vector<int>& vals);   // {1,2,3} -> 1 -> 2 -> 3
std::vector<int> to_vector(const Node* head);
void free_list(Node* head);                  // deletes every node
```

`free_list` must save `next` **before** deleting the node: after `delete head`, reading `head->next` is use after free.

**Worked example: reverse in place.** Keep three pointers: `prev` (the already reversed part), `cur` (the node being moved), `next` (the rest, saved before we cut the link).

```cpp
#include <iostream>

struct Node { int value; Node* next; };

Node* reverse(Node* head) {
    Node* prev = nullptr;
    Node* cur = head;
    while (cur) {
        Node* next = cur->next;  // 1. save the rest
        cur->next = prev;        // 2. point this node backwards
        prev = cur;              // 3. reversed part grows by one
        cur = next;              // 4. move on
    }
    return prev;                 // the old tail is the new head
}

int main() {
    Node* head = new Node{1, new Node{2, new Node{3, nullptr}}};
    head = reverse(head);
    for (Node* p = head; p; p = p->next) std::cout << p->value << ' ';
    std::cout << '\n';                                   // 3 2 1
    while (head) { Node* n = head->next; delete head; head = n; }
}
```

Trace it on 1 → 2 → 3 with a pencil: after each loop, `prev` is a correctly reversed list (1; then 2 → 1; then 3 → 2 → 1) and `cur` is the untouched rest. Empty list: the loop never runs and `nullptr` comes back. That invariant ("prev is reversed, cur is the rest") is what you'll say out loud in an interview.

Compile every task with `g++ -std=c++20 -g -fsanitize=address` so a missing save or an extra delete shows up immediately.

tasks.solution:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;
};

Node* build(const std::vector<int>& vals) {
    Node* head = nullptr;
    for (auto it = vals.rbegin(); it != vals.rend(); ++it) head = new Node{*it, head};
    return head;
}

std::vector<int> to_vector(const Node* head) {
    std::vector<int> out;
    for (; head != nullptr; head = head->next) out.push_back(head->value);
    return out;
}

void free_list(Node* head) {
    while (head) {
        Node* next = head->next;
        delete head;
        head = next;
    }
}

int length(const Node* head) {
    int n = 0;
    for (; head; head = head->next) ++n;
    return n;
}

int main() {
    Node* h = build({1, 2, 3});
    std::cout << length(h) << '\n';
    free_list(h);
}
```

tasks.tests:
Node* h = build({1, 2, 3});
assert((to_vector(h) == std::vector<int>{1, 2, 3}));
assert(length(h) == 3);
assert(length(nullptr) == 0);
free_list(h);
assert(build({}) == nullptr);

tasks.solution:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;
};

Node* build(const std::vector<int>& vals) {
    Node* head = nullptr;
    for (auto it = vals.rbegin(); it != vals.rend(); ++it) head = new Node{*it, head};
    return head;
}

std::vector<int> to_vector(const Node* head) {
    std::vector<int> out;
    for (; head != nullptr; head = head->next) out.push_back(head->value);
    return out;
}

void free_list(Node* head) {
    while (head) {
        Node* next = head->next;
        delete head;
        head = next;
    }
}

Node* reverse(Node* head) {
    Node* prev = nullptr;
    while (head) {
        Node* next = head->next;
        head->next = prev;
        prev = head;
        head = next;
    }
    return prev;
}

Node* reverse_recursive(Node* head) {
    if (!head || !head->next) return head;
    Node* new_head = reverse_recursive(head->next);
    head->next->next = head;
    head->next = nullptr;
    return new_head;
}

int main() {
    Node* h = reverse(build({1, 2, 3}));
    free_list(h);
}
```
The recursive version uses O(n) stack; the iterative one O(1). Both are O(n) time.

tasks.tests:
Node* a = reverse(build({1, 2, 3, 4}));
assert((to_vector(a) == std::vector<int>{4, 3, 2, 1}));
Node* b = reverse_recursive(a);
assert((to_vector(b) == std::vector<int>{1, 2, 3, 4}));
free_list(b);
assert(reverse(nullptr) == nullptr);
Node* one = build({7});
assert(reverse_recursive(one) == one);
free_list(one);

