```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;
};

Node* build(const std::vector<int>& vals) {
    Node* head = nullptr;
    for (auto it = vals.rbegin(); it != vals.rend(); ++it) head = new Node{*it, head};
    return head;
}

std::vector<int> to_vector(const Node* head) {
    std::vector<int> out;
    for (; head != nullptr; head = head->next) out.push_back(head->value);
    return out;
}

void free_list(Node* head) {
    while (head) {
        Node* next = head->next;
        delete head;
        head = next;
    }
}

Node* remove_all(Node* head, int v) {
    Node dummy{0, head};
    Node* p = &dummy;
    while (p->next) {
        if (p->next->value == v) {
            Node* victim = p->next;
            p->next = victim->next;
            delete victim;
        } else {
            p = p->next;
        }
    }
    return dummy.next;
}

int main() {
    Node* h = remove_all(build({3, 1, 3}), 3);
    free_list(h);
}
```
The dummy turns 'the head is special' into 'every node has a predecessor'. It lives on the stack, so it is never deleted.

tasks.tests:
Node* a = remove_all(build({3, 1, 3, 3, 2, 3}), 3);
assert((to_vector(a) == std::vector<int>{1, 2}));
free_list(a);
assert(remove_all(build({3, 3}), 3) == nullptr);
Node* b = remove_all(build({1, 2}), 5);
assert((to_vector(b) == std::vector<int>{1, 2}));
free_list(b);

tasks.solution:
```cpp
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

class IntList {
public:
    IntList() = default;
    ~IntList() { clear(); }
    IntList(const IntList&) = delete;             // copying comes on Wednesday
    IntList& operator=(const IntList&) = delete;

    void push_front(int v) {
        head_ = new Node{v, head_};
        ++size_;
    }
    int pop_front() {
        if (!head_) throw std::out_of_range("pop_front on empty list");
        Node* old = head_;
        int v = old->value;
        head_ = old->next;
        delete old;
        --size_;
        return v;
    }
    int front() const {
        if (!head_) throw std::out_of_range("front on empty list");
        return head_->value;
    }
    std::size_t size() const { return size_; }
    bool empty() const { return size_ == 0; }
    void clear() {
        while (head_) {
            Node* next = head_->next;
            delete head_;
            head_ = next;
        }
        size_ = 0;
    }

private:
    struct Node {
        int value;
        Node* next;
    };
    Node* head_ = nullptr;
    std::size_t size_ = 0;
};

int main() {
    IntList l;
    l.push_front(2);
    l.push_front(1);
    std::cout << l.front() << ' ' << l.size() << '\n';
}
```

tasks.tests:
IntList l;
assert(l.empty() && l.size() == 0);
l.push_front(1); l.push_front(2); l.push_front(3);
assert(l.size() == 3 && l.front() == 3);
assert(l.pop_front() == 3 && l.pop_front() == 2);
assert(l.size() == 1);
l.clear();
assert(l.empty());
bool threw = false;
try { l.pop_front(); } catch (const std::out_of_range&) { threw = true; }
assert(threw);

steps.body:
Until now every sketch **polled**: `loop()` calls `digitalRead()` and hopes to look at the right moment. That fails for short or fast events. A wheel encoder at full speed produces an edge every few milliseconds; if `loop()` is busy printing to Serial for 10 ms, edges are lost.

An **interrupt** lets the hardware call a function the instant a pin changes, pausing whatever `loop()` was doing. That function is an **interrupt service routine (ISR)**. On the Uno only **D2 and D3** have external interrupts.

```arduino
volatile unsigned long count = 0;   // shared with the ISR

void onEdge() {          // the ISR: no arguments, no return value, very short
  count++;
}

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(2, INPUT_PULLUP);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(2), onEdge, FALLING);  // RISING, FALLING or CHANGE
}

void loop() {
  noInterrupts();               // copy the 4-byte counter in one piece
  unsigned long c = count;
  interrupts();
  Serial.println(c);
  delay(500);
}
```

Three rules, each with a reason:

1. **`volatile`** on any variable the ISR changes. Without it, the compiler may keep `count` in a register inside `loop()` and never see the ISR's update.
2. **Copy multi-byte shared variables with interrupts off.** The Uno is an 8-bit CPU: reading a 4-byte `unsigned long` takes several instructions. If the ISR fires halfway, you get half old, half new bytes (for example, reading 255 → 256 as 0 or 511). `noInterrupts()` for the two-line copy prevents that.
3. **Keep the ISR tiny.** No `Serial.print`, no `delay()`. `millis()` does not advance while an ISR runs (it is itself updated by an interrupt), and other interrupts wait. Count, set a flag, maybe store a timestamp; do everything else in `loop()`.

A mechanical button **bounces**: one press makes several fast edges over 1 to 10 ms. Polling code with a delay hides this; an interrupt counts every bounce. The fix inside the ISR is to ignore edges that come too soon after the last accepted one, using `micros()` (reading it inside an ISR is fine for short intervals).

In Wokwi (wokwi.com, new Arduino Uno project):

1. Add a pushbutton. Wire one side to **D2** and the other to **GND**. No resistor: `INPUT_PULLUP` enables the Uno's internal pull-up, so the pin reads HIGH at rest and LOW when pressed, which is why the sketch triggers on **FALLING**.
2. Paste the sketch from the Learn step and run it. Press the button 10 times, slowly. The Serial Monitor should climb by one per press.
3. Add a second counter, `volatile unsigned long polled = 0;`, and in `loop()` poll the same pin with a `delay(500)` and count HIGH→LOW changes you see. Press quickly 10 times within 2 seconds. Compare: the interrupt counter catches every press; the polling counter misses most of them, because it only looks twice per second.
4. Add the debounce version of the ISR:

```arduino
volatile unsigned long lastEdgeUs = 0;

void onEdge() {
  unsigned long now = micros();
  if (now - lastEdgeUs > 5000) {   // accept edges at least 5 ms apart
    count++;
    lastEdgeUs = now;
  }
}
```

Whether the simulated button bounces depends on the part's settings, so the real measurement is what proves the fix. Save the project and copy its share link into your notes: it becomes the students' starter.

