steps.body:
An **encoder** turns rotation into pulses you can count. Two kinds appear on student robots:

- **Optical slot encoder.** A plastic disc with slots (20 is common on the yellow TT-motor kits) turns through a U-shaped sensor: an IR LED on one side, a phototransistor on the other, and an LM393 comparator board that outputs a clean digital signal. Each slot passing gives one pulse. It can't tell direction.
- **Quadrature (Hall or optical) encoder.** Two sensors, A and B, a quarter cycle apart. When A falls, B is HIGH for one direction and LOW for the other. Motors like the N20 or JGA25 with built-in encoders work this way, usually counting on the motor shaft before the gearbox, so ticks per wheel turn = pulses per motor turn × gear ratio.

**From ticks to distance.** Wheel diameter 65 mm: circumference = π × 65 ≈ 204.2 mm. With 20 slots and counting RISING edges, one tick = 204.2 / 20 ≈ **10.2 mm**. Counting CHANGE (both edges) doubles the resolution to 5.1 mm per tick, but only if the slots and bars are about equally wide; otherwise the ticks are uneven.

Counting code is Monday's ISR, one per wheel:

```arduino
volatile long leftTicks = 0, rightTicks = 0;
void onLeft()  { leftTicks++; }
void onRight() { rightTicks++; }

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200);
  pinMode(2, INPUT); pinMode(3, INPUT);   // the LM393 board drives the line
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(2), onLeft, RISING);
  attachInterrupt(digitalPinToInterrupt(3), onRight, RISING);
}

void loop() {
  noInterrupts();
  long l = leftTicks, r = rightTicks;
  interrupts();
  Serial.print(l); Serial.print(' '); Serial.print(r);
  Serial.print("  mm: "); Serial.println(l * 10.2);
  delay(200);
}
```

**Pin plan for this week** (the car from week 12 with a TB6612FNG driver; if your car uses other pins for the driver, keep them, but the encoders must go to D2 and D3, the Uno's only external-interrupt pins):

| Signal | Uno pin |
| --- | --- |
| Left encoder output | D2 (interrupt 0) |
| Right encoder output | D3 (interrupt 1) |
| TB6612 PWMA / AIN1 / AIN2 (left motor) | D5 / D7 / D8 |
| TB6612 PWMB / BIN1 / BIN2 (right motor) | D6 / D12 / D11 |
| TB6612 STBY | D4 |

Predict, without running it, how many times "bye" is printed:

```cpp-snippet
struct Noisy { ~Noisy() { std::cout << "bye\n"; } };

void f() {
    Noisy a;
    Noisy* b = new Noisy;
    Noisy* c = new Noisy;
    delete c;
}
int main() { f(); }
```

And: is anything wrong with the program? If so, what would a tool have to watch for to catch it?

### Asking for memory at run time

`new T` creates a `T` on the heap and returns a pointer to it. `delete p` runs the destructor and gives the memory back. Arrays have their own pair: `new int[n]` and `delete[] p`. Mixing them (`delete` on something from `new[]`) is undefined behaviour.

```cpp-snippet
int* one = new int(7);       // one int, value 7
int* many = new int[100]{};  // 100 ints, all zero
delete one;
delete[] many;
```

You need the heap when an object must outlive the function that creates it, when its size is only known at run time, or when it is too big for the stack. In the pretest, `a` is on the stack, so its destructor runs at the end of `f`; `c` is deleted; `b` is never deleted. "bye" prints twice and `b` **leaks**: its memory is lost until the process ends.

### The three classic bugs

| Bug | What happens | Typical cause |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Leak | memory never freed | an early `return` or an exception skips the `delete` |
| Double free | `delete` twice on the same pointer | two pointers think they own the same object |
| Use after free | reading through a pointer after `delete` | a saved pointer outlives the object |

On your Mac, build with `g++ -std=c++20 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined file.cpp` while learning (`g++` there is Apple clang, which supports the sanitizers). AddressSanitizer stops the program at a double free or use after free and prints the lines of the allocation and the free. Leaks are the exception: Apple clang's AddressSanitizer does not run leak detection on macOS, so use the system tool instead: build without sanitizers and run `leaks --atExit -- ./a.out`, which lists every block still allocated at exit. (On Linux, the same `-fsanitize=address` build reports leaks too.)

### Why manual delete doesn't scale

```cpp-snippet
void process() {
    int* buf = new int[1000];
    if (!load(buf)) return;          // leak
    compute(buf);                    // if this throws: leak
    delete[] buf;
}
```

Every exit path needs its own `delete`. Real code has many exits, so this breaks.

### RAII: tie the resource to an object's lifetime

**RAII** (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) means: acquire the resource in a constructor, release it in the destructor, and keep the owning object on the stack (or inside another owner). C++ guarantees destructors of local objects run however the scope is left (normal end, `return`, exception). So the release happens automatically, exactly once. `std::vector`, `std::string`, `std::fstream` and `std::lock_guard` are all RAII types; that's why you haven't written `delete` yet.

### The rule of three

If your class owns a raw resource, the compiler-generated copy is wrong: it copies the **pointer**, so two objects own one buffer and both destructors free it (double free). The rule of three: if you write a destructor, you almost certainly need a copy constructor and a copy assignment operator too. (The rule of five adds the move constructor and move assignment; Friday's smart pointers make most of this unnecessary, and the best rule is the *rule of zero*: hold resources only in members that already manage themselves.)

### Worked example: a small owning buffer

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

class IntBuffer {
public:
    explicit IntBuffer(std::size_t n) : size_(n), data_(new int[n]{}) {}
    ~IntBuffer() { delete[] data_; }

    // copy constructor: deep copy
    IntBuffer(const IntBuffer& other) : size_(other.size_), data_(new int[other.size_]) {
        std::copy(other.data_, other.data_ + size_, data_);
    }
    // copy assignment: copy-and-swap is safe even for self-assignment
    IntBuffer& operator=(IntBuffer other) {
        std::swap(size_, other.size_);
        std::swap(data_, other.data_);
        return *this;   // 'other' now holds our old buffer and frees it
    }

    std::size_t size() const { return size_; }
    int& operator[](std::size_t i) { return data_[i]; }
    int operator[](std::size_t i) const { return data_[i]; }

private:
    std::size_t size_;
    int* data_;
};

int main() {
    IntBuffer a(3);
    a[0] = 1; a[1] = 2; a[2] = 3;
    IntBuffer b = a;   // copy constructor
    b[0] = 100;        // does not change a
    IntBuffer c(1);
    c = a;             // copy assignment
    std::cout << a[0] << ' ' << b[0] << ' ' << c.size() << '\n';  // 1 100 3
}   // c, b, a destroyed here in reverse order; each frees its own buffer
```

Walk through it: `a` allocates 3 ints. `b = a` allocates a second block and copies the values, so writing `b[0]` leaves `a` alone. `c = a`: the parameter `other` is a copy of `a` (a third block); swapping gives `c` that block and hands `c`'s old 1-int block to `other`, which frees it when the function returns. At the end of `main`, three destructors free three blocks. No leak, no double free. Delete the copy constructor from this class and run it under AddressSanitizer: you'll see "attempting double-free" pointing at `~IntBuffer`.

Two members are declared in the order `size_`, `data_`, and the initializer list uses the same order. Members are always initialized in declaration order, whatever order you write the list in, so keep them matching.

