steps.body:
Two "identical" TT motors are never identical: gearbox friction, magnet strength and battery wiring differ. Give both PWM 180 and one wheel turns a few percent faster, so the car curves. A 3% speed difference on a car with 13 cm between wheels turns it noticeably over a couple of metres.

The encoders tell you the truth. Compare **cumulative** ticks since the start, not per-window counts (Thursday's math lesson showed two perfect wheels differ by a tick in most 100 ms windows; reacting to that is chasing noise).

**Proportional correction.** Every 50 ms:

error = leftTotal − rightTotal (positive means the left wheel is ahead)
left PWM = base − Kp × error, right PWM = base + Kp × error, clamped to 0..255.

The bigger the difference, the harder you push back. This is the **P** of PID, which next week covers properly.

```arduino
const int BASE = 180;
const float KP = 4.0;
unsigned long lastCtrl = 0;

void loop() {
  if (millis() - lastCtrl >= 50) {
    lastCtrl += 50;
    noInterrupts();
    long l = leftTicks, r = rightTicks;
    interrupts();
    long err = l - r;
    int pwmL = constrain(BASE - KP * err, 0, 255);
    int pwmR = constrain(BASE + KP * err, 0, 255);
    setMotors(pwmL, pwmR);   // your week-12 helper: direction pins + analogWrite
  }
}
```

Choosing Kp: too small and drift remains; too large and the car wiggles left-right as the correction overshoots. Start around 2–5 and adjust by halving or doubling.

**Pin plan for this week** (the car from week 12 with a TB6612FNG driver; if your car uses other pins for the driver, keep them, but the encoders must go to D2 and D3, the Uno's only external-interrupt pins):

| Signal | Uno pin |
| --- | --- |
| Left encoder output | D2 (interrupt 0) |
| Right encoder output | D3 (interrupt 1) |
| TB6612 PWMA / AIN1 / AIN2 (left motor) | D5 / D7 / D8 |
| TB6612 PWMB / BIN1 / BIN2 (right motor) | D6 / D12 / D11 |
| TB6612 STBY | D4 |

### Ownership is the question

Wednesday's `IntBuffer` worked, but it took a destructor, a copy constructor and a copy assignment to make one pointer safe. The standard library has already written those classes. A **smart pointer** is an RAII object that owns a heap object and deletes it in its destructor. The choice between them is a choice about **ownership**: who is responsible for deleting this object?

| Type | Owners | Copy? | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `std::unique_ptr<T>` | exactly one | no, move only | same size as a raw pointer, no overhead |
| `std::shared_ptr<T>` | many, counted | yes, copy adds an owner | a control block with an atomic reference count |
| `std::weak_ptr<T>` | none (observes a shared object) | yes | can check whether the object still exists |
| `T*` or `T&` | none | yes | for "I use it, someone else owns it" |

Default to `unique_ptr`. Reach for `shared_ptr` only when ownership is genuinely shared and you can't name a single owner (a texture used by several scene objects, a message held by several subscribers). In ROS 2's `rclcpp`, which you'll meet in the autumn, nodes and messages are routinely passed as `shared_ptr`, so you need to read both fluently.

### unique_ptr

```cpp-snippet
auto p = std::make_unique<Motor>(9, 10);   // create; never write `new` yourself
p->set_speed(120);                           // use like a pointer
Motor& m = *p;                               // or dereference
auto q = std::move(p);                       // ownership moves; p is now nullptr
q.reset();                                   // delete the Motor now; q is nullptr
```

Copying is deleted (`unique_ptr(const unique_ptr&) = delete`), which is exactly what you did by hand to `IntStack` on Wednesday. So in the pretest, (1) does not compile, (2) compiles and leaves `a` as `nullptr`, and (3) compiles and the int now has two owners (`d.use_count() == 2`).

To pass a `unique_ptr`-owned object to a function that only **uses** it, pass `T&` or `T*` (via `*p` or `p.get()`). Pass the `unique_ptr` itself by value only when the function takes ownership (the caller must `std::move` it in, which makes the transfer visible in the code).

### shared_ptr and its counter

Each `shared_ptr` copy increments a count in a shared control block; each destruction decrements it; the object is deleted when it reaches zero. Prefer `std::make_shared<T>(args...)`: one allocation for object plus control block.

The trap is a **cycle**: if A holds a `shared_ptr` to B and B holds one to A, neither count can reach zero, and both leak. Break it by making one direction a `weak_ptr`: it doesn't count as an owner; `w.lock()` returns a `shared_ptr` that is empty if the object is already gone.

### Worked example: a linked list owned by unique_ptr

Each node owns the next node. The list owns the head. Destroying the head destroys the whole chain, so there is no destructor to write, no copy to get wrong.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>

struct Node {
    int value;
    std::unique_ptr<Node> next;
};

class IntList {
public:
    void push_front(int v) {
        auto n = std::make_unique<Node>();
        n->value = v;
        n->next = std::move(head_);   // new node takes ownership of the old chain
        head_ = std::move(n);         // list takes ownership of the new node
        ++size_;
    }
    int size() const { return size_; }
    std::vector<int> to_vector() const {
        std::vector<int> out;
        for (const Node* p = head_.get(); p != nullptr; p = p->next.get())  // raw pointer to observe
            out.push_back(p->value);
        return out;
    }
    ~IntList() {   // iterative teardown: avoids deep recursion on very long lists
        while (head_) head_ = std::move(head_->next);
    }
private:
    std::unique_ptr<Node> head_;
    int size_ = 0;
};

int main() {
    IntList list;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 4; ++i) list.push_front(i);
    for (int v : list.to_vector()) std::cout << v << ' ';
    std::cout << "(size " << list.size() << ")\n";   // 4 3 2 1 (size 4)

    auto a = std::make_shared<int>(42);
    {
        auto b = a;
        std::cout << a.use_count() << '\n';   // 2
    }
    std::cout << a.use_count() << '\n';       // 1
    std::weak_ptr<int> w = a;
    a.reset();
    std::cout << (w.lock() == nullptr) << '\n';  // 1: the int is gone
}
```

Three details. Traversal uses a raw `const Node*` from `.get()`: observing, not owning, so that's the right tool. `push_front` is two moves, and the order matters: move the old head into the new node before overwriting `head_`. And the destructor: the default one would work, but it destroys `head_`, which destroys its `next`, which destroys its `next`... one stack frame per node. A million-node list can overflow the stack. The loop releases nodes one at a time instead; each assignment `head_ = std::move(head_->next)` deletes exactly one node.

`IntList` is not copyable (because `unique_ptr` isn't), which is the right default. If you want copies, you now write one copy constructor that builds new nodes; you still never write `delete`.

